# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_ref-3]  Nauru does not have an official capital, but Yaren is the seat of parliament. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-directory-2]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_ref-7]  English is widely spoken by the majority of the population and it is commonly used in government, legislation and commerce alongside Nauruan. Because of Nauru's history and relationship with Australia,  [LINK: (Australian English) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_English]  is the dominant variety. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-CIA-5]  [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-state-6]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_ref-17]   [LINK: (/nɑːˈuːruː/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:LL-Q1860_(eng)-Flame,_not_lame-Nauru.wav]   [LINK: (nah-OO-roo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Pronunciation_respelling_key]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-:0-15]  or  [LINK: (/ˈnaʊruː/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English]   [LINK: (NOW-roo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Pronunciation_respelling_key] ; [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-:1-16]   [LINK: (Nauruan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauruan_language] : Naoero

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_ref-18]   [LINK: (Nauruan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauruan_language] : Repubrikin Naoero

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_ref-176]  Indirectly influenced by German colonialism, reigen in this cultural context is a German word that implies styles of circular-moving dances in Nauru's historical past, implying that historical Nauruan traditional dances tend to be informal. [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauru#cite_note-175]


